::GEO_U05_L02_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L02_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.5
Question:
A central angle intercepts an arc measuring 40°. What is the measure of the central angle?
{
~A. 20°#Halving the arc measure applies to an inscribed angle, not a central angle.
=B. 40°#A central angle has the same measure as its intercepted arc.
~C. 80°#Doubling the arc measure does not describe a central angle.
~D. Cannot be determined#The intercepted arc measure is given, so the central angle measure can be determined.
}
::GEO_U05_L02_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L02_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.5
Question:
An inscribed angle intercepts an arc measuring 90°. What is the measure of the inscribed angle?
{
~A. 90°#That would be the central angle measure, not the inscribed angle.
~B. 180°#This doubles the arc instead of halving it.
~C. Cannot be determined#The intercepted arc measure is given, so the inscribed angle can be determined by halving the arc.
=D. 45°#An inscribed angle measures half its intercepted arc.
}
::GEO_U05_L02_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L02_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.5
Question:
An inscribed angle measures 35°. What is the measure of its intercepted arc?
{
=A. 70°#The intercepted arc is twice the inscribed angle measure.
~B. 35°#This confuses the inscribed angle with the arc it intercepts.
~C. 17.5°#This halves the angle instead of doubling it to find the arc.
~D. 180°#The intercepted arc is not necessarily a semicircle.
}
::GEO_U05_L02_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L02_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.5
Question:
In the described circle situation, two chords are marked congruent in the same circle. What can be concluded about their intercepted minor arcs?
{
~A. The arcs must add to 180°.#Congruent chords do not necessarily form a semicircle.
~B. One intercepted arc is twice the other.#Congruent chords support equal arcs, not a doubled relationship.
=C. The intercepted minor arcs are congruent.#Congruent chords in the same circle intercept congruent arcs.
~D. No arc relationship can be concluded.#A chord theorem gives a valid arc conclusion.
}
::GEO_U05_L02_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L02_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.5
Question:
In the described circle situation, an angle has its vertex on the circle and intercepts an arc. Which relationship is correct?
{
~A. The angle measure equals twice its intercepted arc.#This reverses the inscribed angle relationship.
=B. The angle measure is half the measure of its intercepted arc.#An angle with vertex on the circle is an inscribed angle, so it is half its intercepted arc.
~C. The angle measure always equals 180°.#Only special cases form a straight angle.
~D. The angle measure equals the circle radius.#Angle measure and radius measure different quantities.
}
